topics.gist:
Whenever two surfaces slide or roll over each other, the tiny peaks (**asperities**) on each surface interlock and even weld momentarily at points of contact. The resistance this creates is **friction**. Friction is not all bad: we need it in brakes, belts, clutches and simply to walk. But between moving machine parts it is a nuisance.

Uncontrolled friction between moving parts causes:
- **Wear and tear** of surfaces, loss of dimensions and early failure;
- **Heat**, which can expand parts, cause seizure, and even local welding;
- **Loss of energy** and so lower efficiency of the machine;
- **Noise** and vibration.

A **lubricant** is any substance placed between two moving surfaces to reduce friction between them; the process is **lubrication**. It works by keeping the surfaces apart (a fluid film) or by putting a low-shear layer between them. So the whole unit of lubricants begins from one idea: replace metal-to-metal (solid) friction with the much smaller internal friction of a lubricant layer.

**Lubricating emulsions** are mixtures of oil and water stabilised by an emulsifier (soap, sulphonated oils). Two kinds exist: **oil-in-water** (oil droplets dispersed in water, used where cooling matters most) and **water-in-oil** (water droplets in oil, used where lubrication matters more, e.g. some steam cylinder and marine applications).

**Cutting fluids** are liquids applied at the tool-workpiece interface during machining (turning, drilling, milling, grinding). Cutting generates intense heat and friction at the tool tip. A cutting fluid:
- **cools** the tool and workpiece, increasing tool life and keeping dimensions accurate;
- **lubricates** the tool-chip interface, reducing friction and power;
- prevents **welding** of chips on the tool (built-up edge);
- **washes away chips**;
- gives a good **surface finish** and prevents **corrosion** of the freshly cut surface.

Types: (i) **soluble oils** (mineral oil + emulsifier, mixed with water to give a milky oil-in-water emulsion): water gives cooling, oil gives lubrication, used for most general machining; (ii) **straight cutting oils** (mineral oil, often with fatty oils or sulphur/chlorine EP additives) for heavy cuts like threading and broaching; (iii) synthetic/chemical fluids.
